<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dominion</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as dominions</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A dominion was a self-governing territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. The dominions had elected parliaments and substantial autonomy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-westminster-1931">Statute of Westminster</a> (1931) granted them full legislative independence. More generally, 'dominions' denotes territories under a ruler's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, as with the inherited lands that formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p></div>
